@* Tasks asks its own module where entries live and the shared
   kernel what is turned on. It conforms to the Inbox's published item contract in
   TasksDrafts, which says so in its own usings rather than here — and it
   never sees Second Brain (Knowledge) or the Shell at all. Now that the context
   is its own project these imports are the project's own rather than inherited
   from the desktop app, so what the pane needs is named here and nothing more. *@
@using System.Globalization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@using Microsoft.JSInterop
@using Backlog.Modules.Tasks.Abstractions
@using Backlog.Modules.Tasks.Abstractions.Services
@using Backlog.SharedKernel
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.GitHub
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Markdown
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Selects
@* SelectionBar, the bulk-action bar above the list. Chrome only - the count,
   the live region, the way out of a selection and the indeterminate select-all
   that .design/interaction-guidelines.md#focus-and-selection asks for - and this
   pane fills its one slot with the selectors already imported above. *@
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Selection
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Feedback
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Badges
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Buttons
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Inputs
@* Modal, for ImportPlanDialog — the pane's one dialog so far. *@
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Overlays
@* SplitPane. Without this the pane rendered a literal <splitpane> element -
   Razor treats an unimported component as unknown HTML rather than failing, so
   the list and the detail pane stacked instead of sitting side by side. *@
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Layout
@* TaskAction is the shape the storybook settled on for "add a step, put it in my
   day, remind me, give it a due date, repeat it" — one control for all of them,
   because a row that is set and a row that is not are two states of one thing. *@
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Tasks
